WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:06.568
Picture a Torrance commission meeting with a thick spreadsheet attached to the agenda.

2
00:00:06.568 --> 00:00:08.852
The title says Annual Progress Report.

3
00:00:08.852 --> 00:00:11.376
One column shows housing-element programs.

4
00:00:11.376 --> 00:00:16.064
Other columns show applications, approvals, permits, affordability, and units.

5
00:00:16.064 --> 00:00:22.554
A commissioner sees a large number of proposed homes and announces that the city has met its housing target.

6
00:00:22.554 --> 00:00:24.297
That conclusion may be wrong.

7
00:00:24.297 --> 00:00:29.886
The APR uses several milestones, and HCD's central RHNA benchmark is issued building permits.

8
00:00:29.886 --> 00:00:34.453
Reading the report well means knowing what each number can and cannot prove.

9
00:00:34.453 --> 00:00:41.830
This lesson is preparation for public service, not legal advice about an APR, enforcement matter, or development proposal.

10
00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:46.667
Housing statutes, forms, reporting instructions, and HCD interpretations change.

11
00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:54.829
Always use the current reporting year instructions, the current statute, the public record, and advice from the Torrance city attorney.

12
00:00:54.829 --> 00:01:03.536
California Government Code requires cities and counties to report annually on implementation of the general plan, including the housing element.

13
00:01:03.536 --> 00:01:11.819
The housing APR covers the prior calendar year and is due to HCD and the Governor's Office of Land Use and Climate Innovation by April 1.

14
00:01:11.819 --> 00:01:14.480
HCD publishes forms, definitions, and tools.

15
00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.745
It also makes submitted data available for public use.

16
00:01:17.745 --> 00:01:19.680
An APR serves several audiences.

17
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.642
Local officials use it to check program delivery.

18
00:01:22.642 --> 00:01:25.484
HCD uses it to monitor compliance and progress.

19
00:01:25.484 --> 00:01:30.079
Residents and advocates use it to compare adopted commitments with outcomes.

20
00:01:30.079 --> 00:01:33.284
Researchers use the data to examine broader patterns.

21
00:01:33.284 --> 00:01:38.665
One spreadsheet therefore carries legal, managerial, and public-accountability functions.

22
00:01:38.665 --> 00:01:40.660
Begin by separating the pipeline.

23
00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.716
An application is not an approval.

24
00:01:42.716 --> 00:01:44.953
An approval is not a building permit.

25
00:01:44.953 --> 00:01:47.493
A building permit is not a completed home.

26
00:01:47.493 --> 00:01:52.813
A certificate of occupancy is not necessarily recorded in the same period as the permit.

27
00:01:52.813 --> 00:01:55.474
Each milestone answers a different question.

28
00:01:55.474 --> 00:01:59.646
Proposed units show market interest and the possible future pipeline.

29
00:01:59.646 --> 00:02:03.153
Approved units show that land-use permission has advanced.

30
00:02:03.153 --> 00:02:09.199
Permitted units are the principal measure HCD uses to track progress toward RHNA by income category.

31
00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:11.013
Completed units show delivery.

32
00:02:11.013 --> 00:02:18.027
Affordable units require attention to the applicable income category and the mechanism that preserves affordability.

33
00:02:18.027 --> 00:02:21.473
Do not add unlike milestones and call the sum production.

34
00:02:21.473 --> 00:02:23.419
Read the report in three passes.

35
00:02:23.419 --> 00:02:25.183
The first pass is arithmetic.

36
00:02:25.183 --> 00:02:31.872
Compare permits with the jurisdiction's RHNA by very low, low, moderate, and above-moderate income categories.

37
00:02:31.872 --> 00:02:36.372
Look at annual numbers and cumulative progress across the planning period.

38
00:02:36.372 --> 00:02:39.960
Ask whether one category is lagging while another advances.

39
00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:42.757
The second pass is geography and project type.

40
00:02:42.757 --> 00:02:46.467
Which neighborhoods and corridors are receiving applications?

41
00:02:46.467 --> 00:02:52.426
How much production is accessory dwelling units, multifamily housing, mixed use, or redevelopment?

42
00:02:52.426 --> 00:02:54.068
Are inventory sites moving?

43
00:02:54.068 --> 00:02:57.230
Are nonvacant sites developing at assumed densities?

44
00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:05.501
Geographic concentration may reveal infrastructure readiness, market preference, displacement risk, or an ineffective program elsewhere.

45
00:03:05.501 --> 00:03:07.508
The third pass is implementation.

46
00:03:07.508 --> 00:03:11.643
Read the program-status entries against the adopted housing element.

47
00:03:11.643 --> 00:03:15.170
Words such as ongoing or in progress are not self-proving.

48
00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:18.028
What action occurred during the reporting year?

49
00:03:18.028 --> 00:03:19.548
Was an ordinance adopted?

50
00:03:19.548 --> 00:03:20.825
Were funds committed?

51
00:03:20.825 --> 00:03:23.440
Did outreach reach the intended population?

52
00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:26.238
Did the city remove the identified constraint?

53
00:03:26.238 --> 00:03:29.400
Does the reported action match the program deadline?

54
00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.832
Check definitions before drawing trends.

55
00:03:31.832 --> 00:03:35.055
A revised form may add fields or change instructions.

56
00:03:35.055 --> 00:03:44.481
A unit may be assigned to an income category through deed restriction, affordability assumptions allowed by current guidance, or another documented method.

57
00:03:44.481 --> 00:03:48.677
Accessory dwelling unit affordability methods have changed over time.

58
00:03:48.677 --> 00:03:51.840
A comparison across years may require normalization.

59
00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.482
Also check for corrections.

60
00:03:53.482 --> 00:03:54.941
APR data can be amended.

61
00:03:54.941 --> 00:03:56.522
A project name may change.

62
00:03:56.522 --> 00:04:01.569
Duplicate records, phased permits, or withdrawn projects can confuse a quick total.

63
00:04:01.569 --> 00:04:08.198
Use the submitted report, HCD's current data, and local permit records together when the distinction matters.

64
00:04:08.198 --> 00:04:10.266
The APR is not merely descriptive.

65
00:04:10.266 --> 00:04:17.321
HCD says it proactively monitors housing-element programs by evaluating APR data and checking in with jurisdictions.

66
00:04:17.321 --> 00:04:20.666
It monitors required rezonings and statutory deadlines.

67
00:04:20.666 --> 00:04:27.661
It also investigates stakeholder inquiries and complaints submitted through the Housing Accountability Unit portal.

68
00:04:27.661 --> 00:04:35.872
HCD may learn of a concern from a housing element, an APR, a comment letter, a phone call, an email, a news article, or another source.

69
00:04:35.872 --> 00:04:41.285
Its published enforcement process generally begins with fact gathering and communication.

70
00:04:41.285 --> 00:04:49.009
Depending on the issue, HCD may monitor, issue a letter of inquiry, provide technical assistance, or request corrective action.

71
00:04:49.009 --> 00:04:56.065
A local government is generally given time to respond, often thirty days, although HCD says timing can vary by case.

72
00:04:56.065 --> 00:05:00.991
If the issue is not corrected, HCD can take stronger action within its authority.

73
00:05:00.991 --> 00:05:10.419
That can include warning that housing-element compliance may be revoked, issuing a notice of decertification, or notifying the California Attorney General.

74
00:05:10.419 --> 00:05:15.041
Other housing laws carry their own remedies, exposure, and court procedures.

75
00:05:15.041 --> 00:05:18.326
Do not treat every HCD letter as the same legal event.

76
00:05:18.326 --> 00:05:23.556
Read the heading, cited authority, requested response, deadline, and stated next step.

77
00:05:23.556 --> 00:05:27.084
Enforcement is not limited to failure to adopt an element.

78
00:05:27.084 --> 00:05:35.234
HCD states that it may review an action or failure to act that is inconsistent with an adopted housing element or housing-element law.

79
00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:37.788
Failure to implement a program can matter.

80
00:05:37.788 --> 00:05:41.559
So can a rezoning that does not deliver the promised capacity.

81
00:05:41.559 --> 00:05:46.121
For a commissioner, the practical response to an HCD letter is disciplined.

82
00:05:46.121 --> 00:05:48.919
Ask staff for the full letter and attachments.

83
00:05:48.919 --> 00:05:51.778
Identify the exact program or statute at issue.

84
00:05:51.778 --> 00:05:55.488
Separate HCD's factual assumptions from its legal conclusion.

85
00:05:55.488 --> 00:06:02.300
Ask what evidence the city will provide, who is preparing the response, and whether a public action is required.

86
00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:05.158
Let the city attorney advise on legal strategy.

87
00:06:05.158 --> 00:06:07.652
Do not improvise a defense from the dais.

88
00:06:07.652 --> 00:06:10.018
Create an APR dashboard with six boxes.

89
00:06:10.018 --> 00:06:13.596
Box one is RHNA progress by income category, using permits.

90
00:06:13.596 --> 00:06:19.783
Box two is the broader pipeline, with applications, approvals, permits, and completions kept separate.

91
00:06:19.783 --> 00:06:22.512
Box three is program deadlines due this year.

92
00:06:22.512 --> 00:06:26.152
Box four is sites-inventory activity and remaining capacity.

93
00:06:26.152 --> 00:06:32.035
Box five is fair-housing and displacement indicators that the element says the city will monitor.

94
00:06:32.035 --> 00:06:37.130
Box six is correspondence, including HCD questions, responses, and corrective dates.

95
00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:39.132
For each box, add a source field.

96
00:06:39.132 --> 00:06:42.528
A number without a source is not ready for deliberation.

97
00:06:42.528 --> 00:06:44.955
Add a date because the pipeline changes.

98
00:06:44.955 --> 00:06:47.987
Add a short note explaining any local methodology.

99
00:06:47.987 --> 00:06:51.020
If an affordability category is estimated, say so.

100
00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:54.538
If the report was corrected, preserve the correction date.

101
00:06:54.538 --> 00:06:57.085
Use the dashboard to ask better questions.

102
00:06:57.085 --> 00:07:05.820
Instead of asking why housing is low, ask which income category is below the expected pace and which adopted program was intended to address it.

103
00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:16.131
Instead of asking whether the city is compliant, ask whether any program deadline is late, whether HCD has raised a concern, and what evidence supports the city's status.

104
00:07:16.131 --> 00:07:20.741
Precision makes the conversation fairer to staff, applicants, and residents.

105
00:07:20.741 --> 00:07:25.229
Remember that high permit counts do not excuse a missed mandatory program.

106
00:07:25.229 --> 00:07:28.080
Low counts do not alone prove unlawful conduct.

107
00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.447
The APR is evidence that must be connected to the governing requirement.

108
00:07:32.447 --> 00:07:34.189
Now three recall questions.

109
00:07:34.189 --> 00:07:35.027
Question one.

110
00:07:35.027 --> 00:07:39.478
What APR milestone is HCD's key benchmark for tracking RHNA progress?

111
00:07:39.478 --> 00:07:39.929
Answer.

112
00:07:39.929 --> 00:07:44.380
Issued building permits, organized by the relevant income categories.

113
00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:45.219
Question two.

114
00:07:45.219 --> 00:07:49.669
When is the housing APR generally due, and what period does it cover?

115
00:07:49.669 --> 00:07:50.121
Answer.

116
00:07:50.121 --> 00:07:55.152
It is due by April 1 to HCD and LCI and reports on the previous calendar year.

117
00:07:55.152 --> 00:07:56.120
Question three.

118
00:07:56.120 --> 00:08:00.570
What are three possible steps in HCD's published enforcement process?

119
00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:01.022
Answer.

120
00:08:01.022 --> 00:08:13.406
Examples include fact gathering, a letter of inquiry, technical assistance, a request for corrective action, a warning of decertification, decertification, or referral to the Attorney General.

121
00:08:13.406 --> 00:08:15.277
The path depends on the case.

122
00:08:15.277 --> 00:08:18.567
Your learner artifact is the six-box APR dashboard.

123
00:08:18.567 --> 00:08:22.437
Populate it with the latest publicly available Torrance APR.

124
00:08:22.437 --> 00:08:27.146
Include a data date, source link, and one verification note in every box.

125
00:08:27.146 --> 00:08:35.015
End with three agenda questions: one about production, one about program implementation, and one about remaining capacity.

126
00:08:35.015 --> 00:08:55.491
This episode draws from the California Department of Housing and Community Development's current Annual Progress Reports page, reporting forms and instructions, APR public data, Housing Accountability Unit enforcement authority and enforcement process pages, and HCD's 2026 housing-law fact sheets and guidance.

127
00:08:55.491 --> 00:09:02.009
It also relies on the applicable California Government Code provisions and public Torrance records.

128
00:09:02.009 --> 00:09:04.642
All explanations are original narration.

129
00:09:04.642 --> 00:09:09.646
No source prose, slide, map, recording, or proprietary data has been copied.

130
00:09:09.646 --> 00:09:11.621
Requirements and forms change.

131
00:09:11.621 --> 00:09:18.271
Confirm the current statute, HCD instructions, local records, and city-attorney advice before acting.
